  "dom_id": "step-q104784",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/e5bcb5723747beb905cc93e3bcf8e3a5be7bf566cf408998d3173490bd2486b5.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{\u2032}(-3)$ where $h(x)=-3f(x)-g(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(x)=-3{f}^{\u2032}(x)-{g}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(-3)=-3{f}^{\u2032}(-3)-{g}^{\u2032}(-3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-5,-2)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-5,-1)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/4f8b5f4c3a87f6cb1c346a4672df96a068d74690f0b7c9c6f9a77a9a3710a34a.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-5,-2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u0394f}{\u0394x}=\\frac{-1-3}{-2-(-5)}=-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(-3)=-\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-5,-1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u0394g}{\u0394x}=\\frac{4-4}{-1-(-5)}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{\u2032}(-3)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(-3) & =-3{f}^{\u2032}(-3)-{g}^{\u2032}(-3) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
